Both answer "what changed since the last version?", but they compare
different things.

An interdiff compares two trees: the tip of v(N-1) and the tip of vN.
It is one ordinary diff, as if you applied each version and diffed
the results. git format-patch --interdiff=<old tip> puts it in the
cover letter under "Interdiff against v(N-1):". It reads easily, but
it has two blind spots. It cannot say which patch a change went
into, and it shows no change to a commit message. It is also only
honest when both versions sit on the same base. If the base moved,
everything that landed in between shows up as if the series had
changed it.

A range-diff compares two series patch by patch. git range-diff
pairs each commit in old-base..old-tip with its counterpart in
new-base..new-tip, then shows a diff of each pair of patches,
commit message included. It marks patches added, dropped, or
reordered. Because it compares patches rather than trees, a rebase
onto a new base does not add noise. The cost is that it is a diff of
diffs, with two columns of +/- markers, so it is harder to read.
format-patch --range-diff=<old range> puts it in the cover letter
under "Range-diff against v(N-1):".

The terminology was not wrong. "Interdiff" is also the general name
for the change between two versions of a patch; patchutils' interdiff
tool, which predates git's, compares two patch files. git uses the
two words for these two specific comparisons. Our tooling follows
that split. With "[PATCH v2 0/4] Require an interdiff on revised
series" [1], libexec/mail/format-patch.sh rebuilds the previous
version from the list and gives an interdiff when that version
applies onto the new base, and falls back to a range-diff when it
does not.

So "versioned patches must have interdiffs" holds in the general
sense, and I would keep it. If you want it precise, "an interdiff,
or a range-diff when the base moved" says what the tool does.

